# What gets uploaded as a Cloud Build context. DENY EVERYTHING, then re-include
# exactly the three trees service/Dockerfile copies.
#
# Written this way round after the allow-list version cost a stalled upload.
# This repository is 8.9 GB: data/legacy is 4.3 GB and cv_garbage - the
# predecessor's photo archive - is another 3.8 GB. An exclude-list has to
# remember every one of those, and the moment somebody drops a new dataset at
# the root it is silently uploaded to a build machine.
#
# THE TRAP THAT CAUSED IT. gcloud falls back to .gitignore ONLY when there is no
# .gcloudignore. Creating this file made gcloud stop reading .gitignore entirely,
# so cv_garbage/ - which .gitignore has excluded for months - became part of the
# build context. The two files answer different questions and neither implies
# the other, so this one names what it wants rather than what it does not.

# Everything at the root, gone.
/*

# The image needs precisely these, and the Dockerfile explains why each:
#   ml/src/sbr     the resolver - imported, never re-implemented
#   ml/configs     crop padding, min box size, the unknown threshold
#   data/taxonomy  streams, form factors, the eleven colours, region packs
#   service/       the service itself
!/ml/
!/data/
!/service/

# data/ is re-included above only so taxonomy can be; legacy is 4.3 GB.
/data/*
!/data/taxonomy/

# The load test is a client, deliberately not in the image.
/service/loadtest/

# Caches, wherever they are. These change on every local run and would bust the
# build cache for no reason even if they were small.
**/__pycache__/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/*.pyc

# Never. Cloud Run gets its configuration as environment variables set at
# deploy time, not baked into a layer.
**/.env
